          <div id="znid-072581897811"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>Long before Dolly stepped onto a Nashville stage, popular culture had flattened Appalachia into a handful of tired images: barefoot hillbillies, hopeless poverty, and people stranded somewhere in the past.</p><p><br></p><p>Dolly knew exactly what the world thought of mountain people. She also knew better.</p><p><br></p><p>“They portray mountain people like we are all these dumb barefoot hillbillies,” she once said. “I think country people are the smartest people in the world, and I’ve been everywhere.”</p><p><br></p><p>Rather than run from the hillbilly label, Dolly grabbed hold of it, bedazzled it, and handed it back on her own terms. She never abandoned her accent, softened her childhood, or treated the mountains as something she had escaped. She became a global icon not despite being Appalachian, but as an Appalachian.</p><p><br></p><p>Without her, the region loses its most famous narrator. But the story does not end with her. It belongs now to the writers, musicians, artists, and ordinary mountain folk willing to keep telling it with the same pride and refusal to be simplified.</p><p><br></p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-251444589275"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="heading"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <h3 id="" class="zn-heading "   >A REGIONAL ECONOMY</h3>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-923151873364"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p><span style="font-family: inherit; font-size: inherit; text-align: inherit; letter-spacing: 0px;">In 1986, Dolly put her name, imagination, and considerable star power behind a small east Tennessee amusement park. Dollywood soon became something much bigger: an economic engine that put food on tables for families across her home county.</span></p><p><span style="font-family: inherit; font-size: inherit; text-align: inherit; letter-spacing: 0px;"><br></span></p><p>Millions of visitors now make their way to Dollywood each year, supporting thousands of jobs as well as the hotels, restaurants, shops, and roadside attractions surrounding it. What Dolly built inside the park gates helped entire communities thrive beyond them.</p><p><br></p><p>And those gates are not closing. In a statement mourning its “Dreamer-in-Chief,” Dollywood announced that the park would remain open because, as Dolly always said, “the show must go on.” The roller coasters will keep climbing, the cinnamon bread will keep baking, and east Tennessee families will keep earning a living from the hometown dream Dolly made real.</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

