          <div id="znid-484633159816"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="heading"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <h3 id="" class="zn-heading "   >A BLUEPRINT FOR SOUTHERN WOMANHOOD</h3>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-151024405331"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>Dolly built a version of Southern womanhood roomy enough to hold apparent contradictions. </p><p><br></p><p>She was glamorous and shrewd, gracious and unyielding, deeply rooted in tradition and entirely self-invented. People saw the wigs, curves, and rhinestones and sometimes missed the formidable businesswoman underneath. Dolly rarely corrected them. Being underestimated could be useful.</p><p><br></p><p>But she knew exactly what her work was worth. She left Porter Wagoner to build a solo career and famously refused to let Elvis Presley record “I Will Always Love You” when his manager demanded half the publishing rights. Later, she turned “9 to 5” into a gleeful battle cry for working women tired of lousy pay and lecherous bosses.</p><p><br></p><p>Dolly hesitated to claim the feminist label, but she never hesitated to claim control over her body, work, and money. “I think women should be treated equally, and I’m going to see to it that I am,” she said in a 2002 interview.&nbsp;</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-874267344930"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="heading"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <h3 id="" class="zn-heading "   >A GLOBAL LITERACY MOVEMENT</h3>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-102859797513"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>What began in 1995 as a book-gifting program for children in Dolly’s Sevier County has grown into an international literacy movement. Dolly Parton’s Imagination Library has now mailed more than 330 million books to children around the world.</p><p><br></p><p>The idea was inspired by her father, Lee Parton, whom Dolly described as one of the smartest people she had ever known. He could not read or write, and she believed that inability had kept him from fulfilling some of his dreams.</p><p><br></p><p>“Before he passed away, my Daddy told me the Imagination Library was probably the most important thing I had ever done,” she said. That praise meant more to her than just about any award.</p><p><br></p><p>Today, the “Book Lady,” as children began to call her, may be gone, but the books will keep coming, carrying Dolly’s dream into millions of homes around the world. “The work ahead has never felt more meaningful,” the Dollywood Foundation said in an online statement, “and we will carry it forward as she lifts us all up.”</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-141043440746"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="heading"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <h3 id="" class="zn-heading "   >A GENRE-BENDING ERA FOR COUNTRY MUSIC</h3>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-779314668055"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>Dolly stretched country music’s borders without snapping its Appalachian roots. She could turn acrylic fingernails into percussion as she did when she conceived the song “9 to 5,” a one-room cabin into a universal story of striving and success, and a farewell song to Porter Wagoner — “I Will Always Love You” — into one of the most enduring love ballads ever written.</p><p><br></p><p>Her influence traveled far beyond country radio. Whitney Houston’s version of “I Will Always Love You” became a pop juggernaut, while “Jolene” has been reimagined by everyone from The White Stripes to Beyoncé. Dolly herself released a disco album in 1978 and a rock album in 2023, plus many of her songs saw dance remixes. </p><p><br></p><p>Yet, through it all, Dolly proved that crossing genres did not require sanding away her accent, abandoning her roots, or becoming less recognizably herself. The country music industry she leaves behind is bigger, quirkier, and more welcoming because she passed through it. </p><p><br></p><p>While no one should be called “the next Dolly Parton,” musically or otherwise, generations of artists, philanthropists, business leaders, and everyday folks will follow the trail of rhinestones she left behind.</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

